1
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,981
This is our planet
's final frontier

2
00:00:50,703 --> 00:00:52,263
An inner world

3
00:00:52,303 --> 00:00:56,224
where only the
most adventurous dare to go

4
00:01:49,872 --> 00:01:51,277
Beneath our feet

5
00:01:51,317 --> 00:01:56,212
are countless
miles of cave shafts and passages

6
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:16,209
The cave of
swallows in Mexico

7
00:02:16,249 --> 00:02:17,689
400 metres
to the bottom

8
00:02:17,729 --> 00:02:21,729
Deep enough to
engulf the Empire State Building

9
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:32,609
This is the
biggest cave shaft in the world

10
00:02:32,649 --> 00:02:35,450
Yet these depths
were first explored

11
00:02:35,490 --> 00:02:38,970
Only two years
before men landed on the moon

12
00:02:42,742 --> 00:02:43,502
Today

13
00:02:43,542 --> 00:02:47,983
caves remain the
least explored places on Earth

14
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:49,384
However

15
00:02:49,424 --> 00:02:51,864
human beings are
seldom the first

16
00:02:51,904 --> 00:02:53,544
to reach
these black

17
00:02:53,584 --> 00:02:55,305
damp places

18
00:03:00,106 --> 00:03:04,827
Here live some of
the strangest and least known animals

19
00:03:04,867 --> 00:03:06,267
on the planet

20
00:03:40,028 --> 00:03:42,748
This galaxy of
little lights

21
00:03:42,788 --> 00:03:46,468
is created by
thousands of living creatures

22
00:03:51,576 --> 00:03:53,742
Any animal that
lives in a cave

23
00:03:53,782 --> 00:03:56,547
has to cope with
complete blackness

24
00:03:56,749 --> 00:03:57,952
But in New Zealand

25
00:03:57,992 --> 00:04:01,920
some have turned
this darkness to their advantage

26
00:04:15,190 --> 00:04:19,111
A silken strand
is lowered from the ceiling

27
00:04:19,311 --> 00:04:21,792
alongside
hundreds of others

28
00:04:29,475 --> 00:04:31,956
Beautiful though
these threads are

29
00:04:32,036 --> 00:04:34,877
they have a
sinister purpose

30
00:04:36,558 --> 00:04:39,882
This is a
cave glowworm

31
00:04:40,283 --> 00:04:41,805
To trap its prey

32
00:04:41,845 --> 00:04:43,167
it goes fishing

33
00:04:43,207 --> 00:04:45,009
with a
line of silk

34
00:04:52,179 --> 00:04:56,464
The silk comes
from glands in the glowworm's mouth

35
00:04:56,545 --> 00:04:59,709
and is loaded
with droplets of mucus

36
00:05:14,342 --> 00:05:18,629
Each glowworm
produces dozens of these threads

37
00:05:21,637 --> 00:05:23,521
Once its
lines are set

38
00:05:23,561 --> 00:05:26,887
the glowworm
hangs from a mucus hammock

39
00:05:26,968 --> 00:05:28,011
and waits

40
00:05:28,051 --> 00:05:30,254
like a
patient angler

41
00:05:34,844 --> 00:05:37,928
But the glowworm
doesn't leave everything to chance

42
00:05:38,209 --> 00:05:40,292
That ghostly blue light

43
00:05:40,332 --> 00:05:42,615
is the result of
a chemical reaction

44
00:05:42,655 --> 00:05:46,820
Taking place
inside a special capsule in its tail

45
00:05:47,942 --> 00:05:51,227
The light literally shines out of its backside

46
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:54,430
It's a lure for
attracting prey

47
00:06:22,421 --> 00:06:26,785
Insects seem
irresistibly drawn towards the source

48
00:06:26,906 --> 00:06:30,630
And then get
trapped by the sticky lines

49
00:06:40,281 --> 00:06:41,642
Once stuck

50
00:06:41,722 --> 00:06:43,564
there is no escape

51
00:06:49,138 --> 00:06:49,778
Now

52
00:06:49,818 --> 00:06:53,301
it's just a
matter of reeling in the line

53
00:06:53,341 --> 00:06:55,863
and slowly
consuming the catch

54
00:06:55,903 --> 00:06:57,064
alive

55
00:07:13,212 --> 00:07:16,416
By ensnaring the
insects that hatch in this cave

56
00:07:16,456 --> 00:07:19,540
these glowworms
have solved the biggest challenge

57
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:21,943
that permanent
cave dwellers face

58
00:07:21,983 --> 00:07:25,507
Finding a regular
and reliable source of food

59
00:07:33,144 --> 00:07:37,747
One kind of rock
makes this whole underground world possible

60
00:07:37,948 --> 00:07:39,429
Limestone

61
00:07:39,830 --> 00:07:42,792
Most of the world'
s caves are found within it

62
00:07:42,832 --> 00:07:45,835
And it covers
nearly 10% of the Earth's surface

63
00:07:52,848 --> 00:07:58,539
Limestone is composed of minerals derived from marine shells and corals

64
00:07:58,579 --> 00:08:02,146
So although this
rocky escarpment in the United States

65
00:08:02,186 --> 00:08:05,231
is now hundreds
of metres above sea level

66
00:08:05,271 --> 00:08:08,236
It was actually
formed underwater

67
00:08:15,641 --> 00:08:19,487
The limestone
towers of Vietnam's Ha Long Bay

68
00:08:19,527 --> 00:08:22,490
are a reminder
of this link with the sea

69
00:08:22,611 --> 00:08:23,372
Originally

70
00:08:23,412 --> 00:08:27,177
this whole area
would have been one solid block of limestone

71
00:08:27,217 --> 00:08:29,459
The base of
a coral reef

72
00:08:40,391 --> 00:08:41,511
In Borneo

73
00:08:41,551 --> 00:08:47,193
rain has sculpted
the limestone into extremely sharp sided pinnacles

74
00:09:01,316 --> 00:09:04,520
But the dissolving power of rainwater

75
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:05,521
has other

76
00:09:05,561 --> 00:09:08,844
much more
dramatic effects underground

77
00:09:20,297 --> 00:09:22,500
Rivers that
flow over limestone

78
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:25,302
often seem to
completely disappear

79
00:09:39,889 --> 00:09:43,529
When the water
reaches a more resistant bed of limestone

80
00:09:43,569 --> 00:09:45,769
Its course
is halted

81
00:09:50,203 --> 00:09:51,642
Once underground

82
00:09:51,682 --> 00:09:53,442
the water
takes on a new

83
00:09:53,482 --> 00:09:55,681
more erosive power

84
00:10:01,581 --> 00:10:04,060
During its
journey from the surface

85
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:07,500
the water absorbed carbon dioxide from the soil

86
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:09,860
making it
mildly acidic

87
00:10:09,939 --> 00:10:11,459
And over
millions of years

88
00:10:11,499 --> 00:10:14,339
this acid eats
away the limestone

89
00:10:14,379 --> 00:10:17,978
creating a maze
of caverns and passages

90
00:10:18,018 --> 00:10:20,778
That sometimes
go on for miles

91
00:11:00,866 --> 00:11:06,265
This is the biggest underground river passage in the world

92
00:11:06,465 --> 00:11:07,425
So big

93
00:11:07,465 --> 00:11:10,584
a jumbo jet
could fly through it

94
00:11:10,664 --> 00:11:13,704
It's Deer
Cave in Borneo

95
00:11:26,294 --> 00:11:32,901
The sheer size of Deer Cave allows some animals to gather there in huge numbers

96
00:11:40,511 --> 00:11:45,757
A staggering three million wrinkledlipped bats live here

97
00:11:49,233 --> 00:11:52,194
The bats roost
high on the walls and ceilings

98
00:11:52,234 --> 00:11:54,875
Where they're
well protected from the outside elements

99
00:11:54,915 --> 00:11:57,036
And safe
from predators

100
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:07,080
And while
they're up here

101
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:10,561
the bats produce
something very important

102
00:12:15,457 --> 00:12:20,902
This hundred metre high mound is made entirely of bat droppings

103
00:12:20,983 --> 00:12:22,023
guano

104
00:12:44,957 --> 00:12:50,288
Its surface is
covered by a thick carpet of cockroaches

105
00:12:51,011 --> 00:12:53,415
Hundreds of
thousands of them

106
00:12:59,068 --> 00:13:02,112
Caves are one of
the few habitats on Earth

107
00:13:02,152 --> 00:13:04,995
not directly
powered by sunlight

108
00:13:05,316 --> 00:13:07,279
In the
absence of plants

109
00:13:07,319 --> 00:13:09,281
this food
chain is based

110
00:13:09,321 --> 00:13:11,444
on a continuous supply

111
00:13:11,484 --> 00:13:13,286
of bat droppings

112
00:13:26,256 --> 00:13:29,058
The cockroaches
feed on the guano

113
00:13:29,579 --> 00:13:32,221
and anything
that falls into it

114
00:13:59,828 --> 00:14:03,714
The droppings
also support other types of cockroaches

115
00:14:03,754 --> 00:14:07,399
which spend part
of their day resting on cave walls

116
00:14:11,871 --> 00:14:14,233
These in
turn become food

117
00:14:14,273 --> 00:14:16,716
for giant
cave centipedes

118
00:14:16,756 --> 00:14:19,718
some more than
20 centimetres long

119
00:14:23,522 --> 00:14:24,483
Bizarrely

120
00:14:24,523 --> 00:14:26,244
there are
crabs here too

121
00:14:26,284 --> 00:14:29,287
sifting through
the droppings for nutrients

122
00:14:36,606 --> 00:14:40,768
All these animals
spend their entire lives within the cave

123
00:14:40,808 --> 00:14:44,931
They're totally
dependent on the digested remains of food

124
00:14:44,971 --> 00:14:47,252
That's brought
in from outside

125
00:15:14,807 --> 00:15:15,568
Each evening

126
00:15:15,608 --> 00:15:17,410
in just two hours

127
00:15:17,450 --> 00:15:21,174
three million
bats leave the safety of the cave

128
00:15:21,214 --> 00:15:24,537
to hunt for
insects in the forest outside

129
00:15:27,341 --> 00:15:29,783
But not all
will return

130
00:15:51,944 --> 00:15:53,426
As they
leave the cave

131
00:15:53,466 --> 00:15:57,631
the stream of
bats form a doughnutshaped ring

132
00:16:00,991 --> 00:16:05,596
The wheeling bats
seem to confuse a rufousbellied eagle

133
00:16:05,636 --> 00:16:08,279
But they must
still survive the attacks of other

134
00:16:08,319 --> 00:16:10,921
more specialised birds of prey

135
00:16:22,512 --> 00:16:24,874
Peregrine
falcons and bat hawks

136
00:16:24,914 --> 00:16:27,996
are the jet
fighters of the bird world

137
00:17:00,476 --> 00:17:03,319
Good hunting will
end as the light fades

138
00:17:03,359 --> 00:17:06,922
So the bat hawks
bolt their catches on the wing

139
00:17:08,548 --> 00:17:11,032
And flies
straight back for more

140
00:17:13,437 --> 00:17:15,641
Any bat separated from the group

141
00:17:15,681 --> 00:17:18,165
becomes a clear
and obvious target

142
00:17:18,205 --> 00:17:19,688
And is
asking for trouble

143
00:17:31,734 --> 00:17:32,135
Yet

144
00:17:32,175 --> 00:17:36,017
the nightly onslaught has little impact on bat numbers

145
00:17:36,057 --> 00:17:37,017
By the morning

146
00:17:37,057 --> 00:17:38,538
the vast majority

147
00:17:38,578 --> 00:17:41,339
will be back in
the safety of the cave

148
00:17:51,666 --> 00:17:56,188
Bats are not the
only commuters in these morning caves

149
00:17:56,228 --> 00:17:58,469
There's a day
shift as well

150
00:18:04,803 --> 00:18:07,084
Returning from
hunting in the sunlight

151
00:18:07,124 --> 00:18:09,685
These commuters
rely on their loud clicks

152
00:18:09,725 --> 00:18:12,447
To find their way
through the cave passages

153
00:18:12,487 --> 00:18:14,087
In total darkness

154
00:18:20,541 --> 00:18:22,821
They're
cave swiftlets

155
00:18:23,221 --> 00:18:24,101
Like bats

156
00:18:24,141 --> 00:18:27,142
they use
echolocation to navigate

157
00:18:27,302 --> 00:18:29,862
We need lights to
see what's going on

158
00:18:29,902 --> 00:18:31,182
But in the
pitch black

159
00:18:31,222 --> 00:18:33,343
the swiftlets
manage unerringly

160
00:18:33,383 --> 00:18:35,983
to locate their
individual nesting sites

161
00:18:36,023 --> 00:18:38,703
Which are only a
few centimetres across

162
00:18:43,852 --> 00:18:45,972
It's a
remarkable skill

163
00:18:46,012 --> 00:18:49,172
and one we still
do not fully understand

164
00:18:53,573 --> 00:18:56,413
These birds are
unusual for another reason

165
00:18:56,613 --> 00:19:01,934
Their little cuplike nests are made entirely from threads of saliva

166
00:19:07,575 --> 00:19:11,175
It takes more
than 30 days to complete one

167
00:19:12,448 --> 00:19:15,208
The nests are
very precious objects

168
00:19:15,248 --> 00:19:17,888
and not only
for the birds

169
00:19:25,453 --> 00:19:26,733
阿桑…

170
00:19:55,091 --> 00:19:57,654
It's a very
risky business

171
00:19:57,775 --> 00:20:00,138
With virtually no
safety equipment

172
00:20:00,178 --> 00:20:03,342
and using ladders
made from forest vines

173
00:20:03,382 --> 00:20:06,907
The gatherers climb into the highest reaches of the cave

174
00:20:06,947 --> 00:20:10,191
Often more than
60 metres from the floor

175
00:20:19,179 --> 00:20:22,021
The work may be
hazardous in the extreme

176
00:20:22,061 --> 00:20:24,543
But the
rewards are great

177
00:20:34,512 --> 00:20:37,314
The pure whiteness of cave swiftlets

178
00:20:37,354 --> 00:20:40,756
Are the main
ingredient of bird's nest soup

179
00:20:40,797 --> 00:20:42,558
And gram for gram

180
00:20:42,598 --> 00:20:44,840
Are worth as
much as silver

181
00:20:47,005 --> 00:20:49,366
As soon as its
nest is removed

182
00:20:49,406 --> 00:20:52,046
a bird will
immediately build another

183
00:20:52,166 --> 00:20:52,606
So

184
00:20:52,646 --> 00:20:56,287
as long as its
valuable harvest is properly controlled

185
00:20:56,327 --> 00:20:59,287
The colonies will
continue to flourish

186
00:20:59,854 --> 00:21:01,255
唔好啊，小北，唔好啊，小北…

187
00:21:01,295 --> 00:21:02,256
唔好啊

188
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:36,840
But water in
caves doesn't only erode

189
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:39,040
it also builds

190
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:49,920
This water is
loaded with dissolved limestone

191
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:52,240
And when it meets
the air in the cave

192
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:55,600
Some of that is
deposited as a mineral

193
00:21:55,720 --> 00:21:57,000
calcite

194
00:21:59,473 --> 00:22:00,673
As it builds up

195
00:22:00,713 --> 00:22:04,914
so the calcite
forms decorations that hang from the ceiling

196
00:22:05,155 --> 00:22:06,675
stalactites

197
00:22:14,637 --> 00:22:19,039
Each drop leaves
behind only a minuscule amount of calcite

198
00:22:19,079 --> 00:22:20,159
But over time

199
00:22:20,199 --> 00:22:23,840
the process can
produce some spectacular results

200
00:22:55,246 --> 00:22:58,012
If the water
seeps through the ceiling quickly

201
00:22:58,052 --> 00:23:01,661
then the calcite
is deposited on the floor of the cave

202
00:23:01,701 --> 00:23:04,507
And that
creates stalagmites

203
00:23:14,272 --> 00:23:17,555
Variations in
water flow and air currents

204
00:23:17,595 --> 00:23:20,157
produce an
infinite variety of forms

205
00:23:20,197 --> 00:23:23,239
but all are
created by the same process

206
00:23:24,041 --> 00:23:28,084
The slow deposition of dissolved limestone

207
00:23:31,527 --> 00:23:34,691
And when stalactite meets stalagmite

208
00:23:34,731 --> 00:23:36,732
a column is born

209
00:24:01,235 --> 00:24:05,117
Structures like
these in North America's Carlsbad Cavern

210
00:24:05,157 --> 00:24:08,478
can take many
thousands of years to develop

211
00:24:08,599 --> 00:24:09,559
But sometimes

212
00:24:09,599 --> 00:24:13,881
the formations in
a cave stop growing altogether

213
00:24:42,383 --> 00:24:44,946
These flooded
caves in Mexico

214
00:24:44,986 --> 00:24:49,230
have remained
virtually unchanged for thousands of years

215
00:24:49,631 --> 00:24:51,233
Since the
last ice age

216
00:24:51,273 --> 00:24:54,436
they've become
cut off from the outside world

217
00:24:54,476 --> 00:24:57,560
Yet their impact
on life at the surface

218
00:24:57,600 --> 00:24:59,041
has been huge

219
00:25:05,016 --> 00:25:06,496
500 years ago

220
00:25:06,536 --> 00:25:10,618
they supported
one of the world's great civilisations

221
00:25:10,819 --> 00:25:11,979
the Maya

222
00:25:17,543 --> 00:25:20,064
Mexico's
Yucatan Peninsula

223
00:25:20,104 --> 00:25:21,185
has no rivers

224
00:25:21,225 --> 00:25:22,986
lakes or streams

225
00:25:23,026 --> 00:25:25,947
So the Maya
relied on the cenotes

226
00:25:25,987 --> 00:25:29,789
the flooded
entrances to the waterfilled caves

227
00:25:33,026 --> 00:25:35,029
These
flooded shafts

228
00:25:35,069 --> 00:25:38,954
are the region's
only source of open freshwater

229
00:25:41,438 --> 00:25:43,641
The cenotes
are in effect

230
00:25:43,681 --> 00:25:46,685
gigantic
freshwater wells

231
00:26:12,356 --> 00:26:15,679
Away from the
lifegiving rays of sunshine

232
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:18,762
One might not
expect to find plants

233
00:26:27,847 --> 00:26:30,408
But in the
darkness of the cave tunnels

234
00:26:30,448 --> 00:26:33,129
roots of giant
tropical trees

235
00:26:33,169 --> 00:26:35,931
have pushed their
way through cracks in the limestone

236
00:26:35,971 --> 00:26:38,172
to reach the
flooded caverns

237
00:26:40,133 --> 00:26:41,534
Without this water

238
00:26:41,574 --> 00:26:45,855
the Yucatan's
forest could not grow so luxuriantly

239
00:27:06,057 --> 00:27:10,102
The Maya knew
that their lives depended on this water

240
00:27:10,142 --> 00:27:13,146
But it's only
with the help of today's technology

241
00:27:13,186 --> 00:27:16,870
That we've come
to appreciate the full significance and scale

242
00:27:16,910 --> 00:27:19,433
Of these
flooded passageways

243
00:27:23,798 --> 00:27:24,838
So far

244
00:27:24,878 --> 00:27:31,237
more than 350 miles of underwater galleries in the Yucatan have been mapped

245
00:27:31,277 --> 00:27:32,276
But still

246
00:27:32,316 --> 00:27:37,636
nobody yet knows
the true extent of this subterranean water world

247
00:27:38,356 --> 00:27:40,315
And with
good reason

248
00:27:43,031 --> 00:27:47,071
Underwater caving
is notoriously dangerous

249
00:27:47,271 --> 00:27:51,151
When the nearest
exit may be hundreds of metres or more away

250
00:27:51,191 --> 00:27:54,791
Running out of
air down here would be fatal

251
00:27:58,431 --> 00:28:00,111
To avoid
getting lost

252
00:28:00,151 --> 00:28:02,992
Divers carry with
them a spool of string

253
00:28:03,032 --> 00:28:04,952
It becomes
their lifeline

254
00:28:04,992 --> 00:28:06,192
literally

255
00:28:20,177 --> 00:28:23,541
The string also
doubles as a measuring tape

256
00:28:23,581 --> 00:28:26,505
A technique that
has been used here in Mexico

257
00:28:26,545 --> 00:28:30,149
To chart the
largest underwater cave in the world

258
00:28:30,189 --> 00:28:32,871
All 100
miles of it

259
00:28:41,199 --> 00:28:43,639
Cave exploration
often requires you

260
00:28:43,679 --> 00:28:47,039
to push yourself
through narrow gaps in the rock

261
00:28:47,560 --> 00:29:02,121
Cavers call such
places "squeezes"，The tighter the squeeze

262
00:29:02,161 --> 00:29:05,241
the greater the
chance of damaging some vital

263
00:29:05,281 --> 00:29:07,361
life
support system

264
00:29:33,817 --> 00:29:35,498
In these
conditions

265
00:29:35,538 --> 00:29:39,059
a diver could
easily become disorientated

266
00:29:39,099 --> 00:29:41,300
and that
could be fatal

267
00:29:55,385 --> 00:30:00,227
The flooded caverns can play tricks on you in other ways

268
00:30:08,547 --> 00:30:10,268
What
seems like air

269
00:30:10,308 --> 00:30:11,348
isn't

270
00:30:12,309 --> 00:30:15,229
It's just
another kind of water

271
00:30:23,073 --> 00:30:25,234
This is a
halo cline

272
00:30:25,274 --> 00:30:28,474
a meeting of
fresh and salt water

273
00:30:31,387 --> 00:30:36,985
Fresh water from the jungle flows over the heavier salt water from the sea

274
00:30:38,507 --> 00:30:42,031
The saltwater
layer is extremely low in oxygen

275
00:30:42,071 --> 00:30:46,036
making it a particularly difficult place for animals to live

276
00:30:46,477 --> 00:30:48,600
Yet some
have managed it

277
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:49,922
like the remipede

278
00:30:49,962 --> 00:30:53,606
one of the most
ancient of all living crustaceans

279
00:31:02,471 --> 00:31:06,555
The Maya understood the importance of the cenotes

280
00:31:06,595 --> 00:31:10,079
But they could never have known that these flooded passageways

281
00:31:10,119 --> 00:31:13,682
were actually the
beginning of subterranean rivers

282
00:31:14,243 --> 00:31:17,646
All of which
eventually flow out to the sea

283
00:31:27,581 --> 00:31:28,541
Saltwater

284
00:31:28,581 --> 00:31:29,943
unlike freshwater

285
00:31:29,983 --> 00:31:32,185
does not
erode limestone

286
00:31:32,225 --> 00:31:34,066
So most sea caves

287
00:31:34,106 --> 00:31:38,189
are created by
the mechanical pounding of the waves

288
00:31:44,803 --> 00:31:49,003
The rocky outcrops of New Zealand's poor night islands

289
00:31:49,043 --> 00:31:50,963
are riddled
with sea caves

290
00:31:51,003 --> 00:31:52,883
and just like
those in Borneo

291
00:31:52,923 --> 00:31:56,523
they have become
important shelters for many species

292
00:32:01,322 --> 00:32:04,122
After a day
feeding in the open water

293
00:32:04,162 --> 00:32:06,482
vast shoals of
demoiselle fish

294
00:32:06,522 --> 00:32:08,322
return to
the caves

295
00:32:08,362 --> 00:32:11,602
which they use as
a refuge from predators

296
00:32:14,318 --> 00:32:15,359
For these fish

297
00:32:15,399 --> 00:32:18,162
the caves are a
nighttime retreat

298
00:32:18,322 --> 00:32:21,245
But they're not
the only commuters in here

299
00:32:21,366 --> 00:32:25,169
There are other
fish working to a different schedule

300
00:32:32,497 --> 00:32:36,061
The big eyes are
the equivalent of bats

301
00:32:36,101 --> 00:32:39,464
Night feeders
that leave the cave each evening

302
00:32:43,768 --> 00:32:46,331
And like all
cave commuters

303
00:32:46,371 --> 00:32:48,053
they are
most vulnerable

304
00:32:48,093 --> 00:32:50,735
at the scheduled
time of departure

305
00:33:02,059 --> 00:33:08,184
A bottleneck funnels these exiting bats into dense concentrations

306
00:33:08,224 --> 00:33:10,666
attracting the
attention of others

307
00:33:38,496 --> 00:33:42,737
The bats can
detect the snakes using echolocation

308
00:33:43,217 --> 00:33:45,778
But the snakes
are literally in the dark

309
00:33:45,818 --> 00:33:47,899
they can
see nothing

310
00:34:01,496 --> 00:34:04,979
The strikes seem
to be largely hit and miss

311
00:34:07,223 --> 00:34:10,306
But the snakes
have a secret weapon

312
00:34:10,827 --> 00:34:14,431
They can actually
sense each bat flying past

313
00:34:14,471 --> 00:34:16,474
Receptors in
the snake's head

314
00:34:16,514 --> 00:34:19,596
Pick up the heat
given off by the flying bats

315
00:34:20,358 --> 00:34:22,760
As this
thermal image shows

316
00:34:29,558 --> 00:34:30,718
To the snakes

317
00:34:30,758 --> 00:34:33,319
the bats are
apparently glowing

318
00:34:33,359 --> 00:34:36,159
and this gives
them something to aim at

319
00:34:58,811 --> 00:35:02,893
This is the price
that these cave commuters must pay

320
00:35:02,933 --> 00:35:06,055
for their
daytime sanctuary underground

321
00:35:06,415 --> 00:35:07,776
Small wonder then

322
00:35:07,816 --> 00:35:11,658
that there are
other cave dwellers that stay put

323
00:35:19,359 --> 00:35:21,799
Many caves
are like islands

324
00:35:21,839 --> 00:35:24,000
cut off from
the outside world

325
00:35:24,040 --> 00:35:26,040
and from
other caves

326
00:35:34,322 --> 00:35:36,003
This isolation

327
00:35:36,043 --> 00:35:41,044
has resulted in
the evolution of some very strange creatures

328
00:35:43,227 --> 00:35:45,872
They are the
cave specialists

329
00:35:45,912 --> 00:35:47,435
troglobites

330
00:35:47,475 --> 00:35:51,763
animals that never emerge from the caves or see daylight

331
00:36:05,016 --> 00:36:07,781
These troglodytes from Thailand

332
00:36:07,821 --> 00:36:11,627
are possibly the
most specialised creatures on Earth

333
00:36:11,667 --> 00:36:15,232
For they live
only in cave waterfalls

334
00:36:16,195 --> 00:36:19,801
The entire population of these cave angel fish

335
00:36:19,841 --> 00:36:24,007
seems to be
restricted to just two small caves

336
00:36:28,114 --> 00:36:30,716
It's the same
story with other troglodytes

337
00:36:30,756 --> 00:36:32,677
There may well be
less than a hundred

338
00:36:32,717 --> 00:36:35,999
Texas cave
salamanders in the wild

339
00:36:52,964 --> 00:36:55,847
And the
Belizean white crab

340
00:36:55,887 --> 00:36:57,008
is another creature

341
00:36:57,048 --> 00:37:00,771
that is unique
to just one cave system

342
00:37:05,656 --> 00:37:07,859
Living in
perpetual darkness

343
00:37:07,899 --> 00:37:11,142
they have all not
only lost the pigment in their skin

344
00:37:11,182 --> 00:37:13,023
but also
their eyes

345
00:37:15,313 --> 00:37:19,034
It takes thousands of generations for eyes to be lost

346
00:37:19,074 --> 00:37:21,795
So these species
must have been isolated

347
00:37:21,835 --> 00:37:23,956
for a very
long time

348
00:37:29,118 --> 00:37:34,600
But the blind salamander has other highly developed sensory organs

349
00:37:37,436 --> 00:37:43,160
Receptors in the skin detect minute movements in the water made by its prey

350
00:37:50,086 --> 00:37:55,971
External gills help it to breathe in water that is particularly low in oxygen

351
00:38:07,794 --> 00:38:12,914
The cave angelfish feed on bacteria in the fastflowing water

352
00:38:12,954 --> 00:38:17,234
Keeping their
grip with microscopic hooks on their fins

353
00:38:28,171 --> 00:38:30,814
Food is often
in short supply

354
00:38:30,854 --> 00:38:33,177
And troglodytes like the crab

355
00:38:33,217 --> 00:38:37,302
Have to survive
on whatever washes into the cave from outside

356
00:38:41,948 --> 00:38:45,713
A salamander might not encounter food for several months

357
00:38:45,753 --> 00:38:47,996
So when something does come along

358
00:38:48,036 --> 00:38:49,998
It can't
afford to miss it

359
00:38:56,991 --> 00:39:02,637
It's astonishing that these extraordinary cave dwellers managed to survive at all

360
00:39:03,879 --> 00:39:06,522
But one cave is
so inhospitable

361
00:39:06,562 --> 00:39:10,325
that one would
not expect it to contain any life whatsoever

362
00:39:16,412 --> 00:39:20,696
The water flowing
out of the Via Luce cave in Mexico

363
00:39:20,737 --> 00:39:24,660
is actually
coloured white with sulphuric acid

364
00:39:39,855 --> 00:39:42,819
Explorers
entering this dangerous cave

365
00:39:42,859 --> 00:39:46,584
must wear respirators and carry monitors

366
00:39:46,665 --> 00:39:50,150
Poisonous gases
rise to fatal levels so quickly

367
00:39:50,190 --> 00:39:53,434
that an early
warning system is essential

368
00:39:58,267 --> 00:40:01,995
Ats survive by
staying close to the skylights

369
00:40:02,035 --> 00:40:04,239
But venturing
deep into the cave

370
00:40:04,279 --> 00:40:06,402
is very
dangerous indeed

371
00:40:16,416 --> 00:40:21,982
The source of these toxic fumes lies several miles below

372
00:40:22,463 --> 00:40:27,709
Hydrogen sulphide gas bubbles out from oil deposits in the Earth's crust

373
00:40:27,830 --> 00:40:33,075
It mixes with oxygen in the water and forms sulphuric acid

374
00:40:44,422 --> 00:40:48,705
These are not the sort of conditions in which you would expect to find fish

375
00:40:48,906 --> 00:40:52,069
Yet these cave
mollies seem to thrive

376
00:40:52,109 --> 00:40:55,512
despite the acid
and the low levels of oxygen

377
00:40:58,195 --> 00:41:02,359
There is in fact
more life here than anyone would think possible

378
00:41:02,399 --> 00:41:07,284
But the biggest
surprise is something altogether more bizarre

379
00:41:17,915 --> 00:41:21,881
These strange
stalactitelike formations

380
00:41:21,921 --> 00:41:22,762
are known

381
00:41:22,802 --> 00:41:24,164
rather
appropriately

382
00:41:24,204 --> 00:41:26,286
as snotites

383
00:41:26,648 --> 00:41:29,252
The drops
dripping from the ends

384
00:41:29,292 --> 00:41:31,133
are
sulphuric acid

385
00:41:31,214 --> 00:41:33,657
strong
enough to burn skin

386
00:41:37,207 --> 00:41:41,650
The snotites are
in fact vast colonies of bacteria

387
00:41:41,691 --> 00:41:44,893
capable of
growing a centimetre a day

388
00:41:49,538 --> 00:41:51,540
In this world
without sunlight

389
00:41:51,580 --> 00:41:56,584
these bacteria extract energy from the hydrogen sulphide gas

390
00:42:01,591 --> 00:42:05,712
Bacteria like
these are known as extremophiles

391
00:42:05,752 --> 00:42:10,232
Because of their
ability to survive in such extreme conditions

392
00:42:12,912 --> 00:42:17,993
And these extremophiles play another important role in this cave

393
00:42:18,033 --> 00:42:19,153
Surprisingly

394
00:42:19,193 --> 00:42:21,674
they are the
basis of a food chain

395
00:42:21,714 --> 00:42:23,914
Which supports
amongst other creatures

396
00:42:23,954 --> 00:42:26,314
the larvae of
these midges

397
00:42:36,423 --> 00:42:40,222
Via Lueh's ecosystem was certainly very remarkable

398
00:42:40,262 --> 00:42:45,462
But cave explorers were soon to make an even more astonishing discovery

399
00:42:58,460 --> 00:43:03,700
Beneath this arid
landscape lies a subterranean wonderland

400
00:43:09,939 --> 00:43:11,179
Without water

401
00:43:11,219 --> 00:43:13,699
one might not
expect to find any caves

402
00:43:13,739 --> 00:43:18,259
But beneath these
rolling desert slopes in the United States

403
00:43:18,299 --> 00:43:20,179
Lies one of
the longest

404
00:43:20,219 --> 00:43:21,259
deepest

405
00:43:21,299 --> 00:43:24,659
and most surprising caves in the world

406
00:43:29,195 --> 00:43:33,316
Its secrets
remained unknown until 1986

407
00:43:33,516 --> 00:43:37,037
When cavers dug
through several metres of loose rock

408
00:43:37,077 --> 00:43:39,237
At the
bottom of this pit

409
00:43:42,718 --> 00:43:44,319
They
named the cave

410
00:43:44,359 --> 00:43:45,599
Leticia

411
00:43:45,919 --> 00:43:47,999
And since
its discovery

412
00:43:48,039 --> 00:43:52,880
More than 120
miles of passageways have been mapped

413
00:43:58,332 --> 00:44:00,614
When the first
explorers descended

414
00:44:00,654 --> 00:44:04,256
no one guessed at
the sheer size of this cave

415
00:44:04,296 --> 00:44:08,018
But even that was
not going to be the biggest surprise

416
00:44:08,459 --> 00:44:10,781
Little did they
realise that Lechuguilla

417
00:44:10,821 --> 00:44:14,063
would soon be
regarded by cavers the world over

418
00:44:14,103 --> 00:44:17,705
as the most
beautiful of all caves

419
00:44:17,945 --> 00:44:19,546
They were
about to discover

420
00:44:19,586 --> 00:44:22,348
some of the most
exquisite formations

421
00:44:22,388 --> 00:44:24,589
ever seen
underground

422
00:44:39,049 --> 00:44:40,771
The walls
were covered

423
00:44:40,811 --> 00:44:44,295
with the most
delicate and fragile crystals

424
00:44:59,970 --> 00:45:03,213
Many of these
crystals were made of gypsum

425
00:45:03,253 --> 00:45:06,175
a mineral that
comes from limestone

426
00:45:06,255 --> 00:45:09,418
And there was
mile after mile of them

427
00:45:26,875 --> 00:45:30,400
Water is the
creator of most caves

428
00:45:30,482 --> 00:45:33,247
But unlike all
other limestone caves

429
00:45:33,287 --> 00:45:38,496
Lechuguilla's rock had not been eaten away by running rainwater

430
00:45:40,180 --> 00:45:42,664
Something else
was responsible

431
00:45:50,944 --> 00:45:53,385
The only water
Lechuguilla has

432
00:45:53,425 --> 00:45:55,546
are these
wonderfully still

433
00:45:55,586 --> 00:45:57,266
clear pools

434
00:46:02,769 --> 00:46:05,491
As the explorers
went deeper into the cave

435
00:46:05,531 --> 00:46:07,692
They came across
whole galleries

436
00:46:07,732 --> 00:46:10,574
Filled with the
most unusual formations

437
00:46:10,614 --> 00:46:12,776
Like these
five metre cones

438
00:46:12,816 --> 00:46:15,697
Frosted with the
most delicate crystals

439
00:46:45,993 --> 00:46:48,873
It was Lechuguilla
's gypsum crystals

440
00:46:48,913 --> 00:46:52,833
That made scientists question how these caverns were formed

441
00:46:55,682 --> 00:46:56,724
They discovered

442
00:46:56,764 --> 00:46:58,726
that Lechuguilla's limestone

443
00:46:58,766 --> 00:47:02,890
had actually been
eaten away by sulphuric acid

444
00:47:02,930 --> 00:47:06,454
cutting through
literally miles of limestone

445
00:47:24,382 --> 00:47:27,702
And when sulphuric acid dissolves limestone

446
00:47:27,742 --> 00:47:29,821
it leaves
behind gypsum

447
00:47:29,861 --> 00:47:33,701
the basis of
Lechuguilla's remarkable formations

448
00:47:33,980 --> 00:47:35,500
And there
was one set

449
00:47:35,540 --> 00:47:37,900
more than a mile
from the surface

450
00:47:37,940 --> 00:47:40,379
that almost
defied belief

